  [BUG] lib/sbi/riscv_locks.c:59-66  spin_lock
      The Zalrsc-only ticket-increment path (LR.W/SC.W loop at lines 59-66) has only two of the five required constrained-LR/SC termination conditions: successful SC and remote-store/AMO breaking the reservation. It lacks a branch-on-timeout exit, a trap-based termination path (interrupts are disabled in M-mode), and any bounded-retry mechanism. If hardware provides basic Zalrsc without a forward-progress guarantee, repeated SC failures under multi-hart contention can cause unbounded livelock. This is the primary M-mode spinlock, making any livelock here catastrophic for the entire system.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/riscv_locks.c:30-39  spin_trylock
      The Zalrsc-only trylock LR.W/SC.W loop (lines 30-39) has a branch exit: `bne %1, %2, 2f` at line 35 breaks out of the loop when the lock is detected as held. This provides a third termination condition (branch exit) beyond successful-SC and remote-store-breaking-reservation. However, the branch exit only triggers after the lock state changes — if the lock remains free and SC keeps failing, the loop retries indefinitely without a timeout or backoff. In practice, trylock contention resolves quickly because one hart's eventual SC success changes the lock state, enabling the branch exit for all other contenders. Risk is lower than spin_lock but still relies on the implicit assumption that at least one hart's SC will succeed
  [BUG] lib/sbi/riscv_atomic.c:47-65  atomic_add_return
      The Zalrsc-only LR.W/SC.W (or LR.D/SC.D) loop at lines 50-53 (and 58-61 for rv64) uses a bare `bnez %2,1b` retry with no timeout, no backoff, and no trap-based termination. Only two termination conditions exist: successful SC and another hart's store breaking the reservation. This primitive underpins reference counting throughout OpenSBI — a livelock here can stall core data-structure operations (e.g., sbi_domain refcounts, PMP counter updates). The compiler-inserted atomic_add/sub builtins may also expand to this loop in Zalrsc-only builds
  [BUG] lib/sbi/riscv_atomic.c:110-128  __axchg
      The Zalrsc-only atomic exchange macro uses LR.W/SC.W or LR.D/SC.D loops (lines 113-115 and 122-124) with bare `bnez %2, 1b` retry. No timeout, no backoff, no trap-based termination. This macro backs `atomic_xchg`, `atomic_raw_xchg_uint`, and `atomic_raw_xchg_ulong` — all core atomic-exchange operations used in spinlock release paths and other synchronization. A livelock here prevents lock release, cascading into system-wide deadlock
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:65-70  _start
      The boot-lottery Zalrsc LR.W/SC.W loop (lines 66-69) has no timeout or branch exit from the SC-failure retry path. The `bnez t0, _wait_for_boot_hart` at line 70 is only reached AFTER SC succeeds, not during retry. In practice this runs exactly once per hart at cold boot with a bounded number of contenders (N harts), and one SC always succeeds because the hardware serializes cache-line ownership. However, the SC-failure retry loop `_sc_fail: lr.w / sc.w / bnez t1, _sc_fail` has only successful-SC and remote-store as termination conditions. If hardware's LR/SC implementation permits indefinite SC failure (permitted under the pre-normative-rule Zalrsc spec), NO hart wins the lottery and the system hangs at initialization
  [RISK] firmware/payloads/test_head.S:37-43  _start
