Spec: Monolithic forward-progress rule split into five independent sub-guarantees; implementations must support ALL termination paths (successful SC, remote store/AMO, branch exit, trap) or risk permanent deadlock in constrained LR/SC loops
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:200-210  atomic_cmpset_32
      Unbounded constrained LR/SC retry loop (lr.w → bne → sc.w → bnez 0b) with no bounded retry count or timeout. Forward progress depends entirely on the hardware providing all five Zalrsc termination conditions (successful SC, remote store/AMO breaking reservation, branch exit, traps, other mechanism). If a non-compliant hardware implementation omits any termination path — e.g., never breaks the reservation on another hart's store — this loop spins forever, causing a denial of service. The invariant 'hardware is Zalrsc-compliant' is assumed but never checked at runtime (no ISA string probing, no bounded iteration). This is correct per the ISA contract but represents a defense-in-depth gap.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2868/

  [OK] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:223-236  atomic_fcmpset_32
      Single-pass LR/SC CAS with no retry loop. After sc.w, the code uses 'j 2f' (unconditional forward jump) rather than branching back to the LR. On SC failure (reservation lost), res is non-zero and the function returns false to the caller, which handles retry at a higher level. All three software-visible exit paths are correctly handled: SC success (res=0 via sc.w), SC failure/reservation loss (res!=0, j 2f, returns false), and comparand mismatch (bne to 1f, saves old value via sw, returns false). No unbounded-loop risk here.
